## Configuration

### Custom Transport Options

```rust file
use {{packageName}}::auth::Authenticator;
use {{packageName}}::{{clientClassName}};
use {{packageName}}::{ConfigurationError, TransportOptions};

fn new_client(authenticator: Box<dyn Authenticator>) -> Result<{{clientClassName}}, ConfigurationError> {
    let transport = TransportOptions::builder()
        .proxy("http://proxy:3128")
        .timeout(Some(5000))
        .build()?;

    Ok({{clientClassName}}::new(authenticator, Some(transport)))
}
```

### Retries

A call is retried up to `max_retries` times (default 2; `.max_retries(0)` disables it) after a network error before a response, and after a status of 408, 409, 429 or 500-599 except 501. The wait is 0.5 s doubling to 8 s with 25% jitter, or the `Retry-After` header (at most 60 s). Each retry is a fresh request with its authentication headers rebuilt, all inside the one `timeout`; a multipart body and a single-use `BodySource` are never retried.

```rust file
use {{packageName}}::{ConfigurationError, TransportOptions};

fn new_transport() -> Result<TransportOptions, ConfigurationError> {
    TransportOptions::builder().max_retries(0).build()
}
```

### Streaming

An operation whose success response is binary (an octet stream, an image, `type: string, format: binary`) returns a `ByteStream` instead of the whole body in memory, so a large download never has to fit in memory. The call returns as soon as the response headers arrive, and the body is read as you consume it. A `ByteStream` has the status code, the lower-cased headers and an idempotent `close()` that aborts the connection instead of draining it, so close it when you stop early (dropping it does the same). `read_all()` reads the rest into memory and leaves the stream closed.
{{#hasBinaryStreamOperation}}

